@document.meta
title: Rebuilding strategy for the onboarding module
branch: review/buddy-all
constraint: preserve API surface exactly — same paths, verbs, request/response DTOs
@end

* Principles

The API surface stays identical throughout.  The work is purely internal
reorganization: same controllers (constructor injection only), same DTOs, same
module API interfaces, same repositories, same entities.  Only the service layer
changes shape.

* Core problems

**1. Monolithic BuddyToolExecutor (549 lines)**

One class with one private method per tool, each returning a plain-text string,
plus hand-rolled JSON-schema specs as constants.  Adding a tool touches this
file in 4 places.  It is the single biggest structural smell.

**2. BoardService (673 lines) does two unrelated jobs**

It manages board/card CRUD (rows, ordering, dismissal) /and/ composes live
content for 11 card types, pulling from 8+ dependencies.  These are different
concerns that change for different reasons.

**3. AI orchestration is copy-pasted across services**

~BuddyService~, ~TaskOrientationService~, ~StarterWorkTaskProposalService~, and
~BoardDiagramService~ each call ~OnboardingAiClient~ with similar patterns
(streaming SSE relay, ~done~-event persistence, error handling) but no shared
abstraction.

**4. BuddyActionService (733 lines) is a grab-bag**

It confirms buddy-proposed actions — assigning Task 0, claiming a goal,
recording a competency assessment, filing an attestation, setting a GitHub
login.  Each action is a distinct domain concern glued into one class.

**5. "Read from the same service as the buddy tool" is enforced by comment**

~BoardService~'s class doc says each card's content comes from the same service
the equivalent buddy tool reads.  That is the right rule, but nothing enforces
it.  A new card added by someone who does not know the rule will drift.

* Step 1 — BuddyTool plugin system

Extract each buddy tool into its own small class behind a shared interface.

#+begin_src kotlin
interface BuddyTool {
    val name: String
    val description: String
    val parameters: JsonObject  // JSON-schema of arguments

    /** True when this tool has something to say for this hire. */
    fun appliesTo(userId: UUID): Boolean

    /** Execute the tool, scoped strictly to the given hire. */
    fun execute(userId: UUID): String
}
#+end_src

Each tool becomes its own class (~20–60 lines): ~GetArrivalStepsTool~,
~GetMyMetricsTool~, ~GetOpenPullRequestsTool~, ~SearchCanonicalAnswersTool~,
~GetTeammatesTool~, ~GetSuggestedTasksTool~, ~GetCompetenciesToAssessTool~,
~GetMyCompetenciesTool~, and each board tool (~NoteTool~, ~ChecklistTool~,
~LinkTool~).

~BuddyToolExecutor~ becomes a thin registry:
- collects all ~BuddyTool~ beans via constructor injection
- ~toolSpecs(userId)~ iterates, calls ~appliesTo~, returns specs for those that
  pass
- ~execute(name, userId)~ finds the matching tool, delegates
- ~stateSnapshot(userId)~ iterates each tool in order, appends its output

This also makes the "read from the same service" rule structural — the board
card composer and the buddy tool both inject the /same/ small domain service
(e.g. ~ArrivalStepService~, ~OnboardingMetricsService~), and there is no
monolithic class to drift from.

Tests: each tool gets its own focused test (~50–100 lines).  The registry test
verifies wiring (all tools discovered, appliesTo gating, execution dispatch).

* Step 2 — Board card composition split

Split ~BoardService~ into three pieces.

**~BoardService~ (stays, shrinks to ~150 lines)**

Owns board CRUD, card ordering, dismissal.  No content composition — it
delegates that to the assembler.

**~BoardCardComposer~ interface**

#+begin_src kotlin
interface BoardCardComposer {
    val kind: BoardCardKind

    /** Compose this card's live content for a hire on a project,
     *  or null when the card does not apply right now. */
    fun compose(userId: UUID, projectId: UUID): BoardCardContent?
}
#+end_src

One implementation per card kind:
- ~PathToFirstContributionComposer~ — injects ~OnboardingMetricsService~
- ~ArrivalStepsComposer~ — injects ~ArrivalStepService~
- ~OpenPullRequestsComposer~ — injects ~OpenPullRequestReader~
- ~CurrentTaskComposer~ — injects ~CurrentTaskReader~
- ~SuggestedTasksComposer~ — injects ~StarterWorkTaskProposalService~
- ~CompetencyProgressComposer~ — injects ~MyCompetencyService~
- ~MemoryRecapComposer~ — injects ~BuddySessionRepository~
- ~DiagramComposer~ — injects ~BoardDiagramService~
- ~NoteComposer~/~LinkComposer~/~ChecklistComposer~ — read from card payloads

**~BoardAssembler~ (new)**

Iterates the composers in order, builds the ~BoardResponse~.  The composers
/are/ the card catalog; adding a card type means adding a new composer bean.

The convention becomes mechanical — a composer cannot drift from the buddy tool
because it has no other way to get the data.  Both inject the same domain
service.

* Step 3 — Shared AI streaming execution pattern

There is a recurring pattern across 4 services: call an AI endpoint, relay SSE
chunks to the browser, persist the result on ~done~.  Extract it once.

#+begin_src kotlin
class AiOperationRunner(
    private val onboardingAiClient: OnboardingAiClient,
) {
    suspend fun <T> runStreaming(
        operation: String,
        request: Any,
        onItem: (JsonElement) -> Unit,      // validate + persist each item
        onDone: (T) -> Unit,                 // persist the final result
    ): Flow<AiProgressEvent>
}
#+end_src

The runner owns:
- relay of ~stage~/~item~/~warning~ chunks
- ~done~ persistence (decoding ~result~ into ~T~, calling ~onDone~)
- ~error~ synthesis when the AI stream fails
- ~warning~ synthesis when an item shown live is rejected on persist

Each calling service supplies only what is domain-specific: the request body,
the item validator, and the persist function.  The streaming relay and error
handling live in one place.

* Step 4 — BuddyActionService split by action domain

Each action the buddy can propose becomes its own small handler.

#+begin_src kotlin
interface BuddyActionHandler {
    val actionType: BuddyActionType
    suspend fun handle(request: BuddyActionRequest, jwt: Jwt): BuddyActionResponse
}
#+end_src

One handler per action:
- ~StartTaskZeroHandler~ — injects ~TaskZeroService~
- ~ClaimGoalHandler~ — injects ~UserGoalService~
- ~RecordAssessmentHandler~ — injects ~CompetencyPlacementService~
- ~RequestAttestationHandler~ — injects ~AttestationService~
- ~SetGithubLoginHandler~ — injects ~UserApi~
- ~FlagToPmHandler~ — injects ~KnowledgeBaseService~

~BuddyActionService~ becomes ~BuddyActionDispatcher~: finds the right handler
by ~actionType~ and delegates.  Each handler gets exactly the dependencies it
needs, and each is testable in isolation.

* Step 5 — Leave the rest alone

These services are already focused and reasonably sized.  They stay as-is:

- ~ArrivalStepService~ (383 lines)
- ~OnboardingMetricsService~ (200 lines)
- ~AttestationService~ (184 lines)
- ~KnowledgeBaseService~ (199 lines)
- ~TaskZeroService~ (157 lines)
- ~CompetencyPlacementService~ (231 lines)
- ~MyCompetencyService~ (75 lines)
- ~RampService~ (282 lines)
- ~BoardDiagramService~ (270 lines)
- ~StarterWorkTaskProposalService~ (565 lines) — this one is large but focused
  on one domain; splitting it would be a separate exercise
- ~TaskOrientationService~ (513 lines) — same assessment

* What changes — and what does not

| Layer            | Changes                                                   |
|------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
| Controllers      | Constructor injection updates only.  Zero endpoint changes. |
| DTOs             | No changes.                                                |
| Module API ifaces | No changes.                                                |
| Repositories     | No changes.                                                |
| Entities         | No changes.                                                |
| Services blown up | ~BuddyToolExecutor~ → ~BuddyToolRegistry~ + ~10 ~BuddyTool~ impls.  ~BoardService~ → ~BoardService~ + ~BoardAssembler~ + ~11 ~BoardCardComposer~ impls.  ~BuddyActionService~ → ~BuddyActionDispatcher~ + ~6 ~BuddyActionHandler~ impls. |
| New shared       | ~AiOperationRunner~ extracted from the 4 AI-calling services. |

Total line count probably goes up slightly (more files, same logic), but the
maximum file size drops from 733 to ~200.  Every class gets one reason to
change.

* Order of work

**1. ~BuddyTool~ interface + impls (first)**

These are leaf nodes with no downstream coupling.  Easy to test, easy to migrate
one at a time.  ~BuddyController~ is the only consumer and it calls through a
single registry — swap the registry and it never knows.

**2. ~AiOperationRunner~ (second)**

Pure extraction.  The four callers swap their ~OnboardingAiClient~ calls for
runner calls with the same request body and the same persist logic.  Should be
behavior-preserving by construction.

**3. Board composition split (third)**

Depends on the same domain services the tools use, so it naturally follows step
1.  The board controller gets ~BoardAssembler~ instead of ~BoardService~ for
content reads and ~BoardService~ for CRUD; the two concerns were already
separate in the callsites.

**4. ~BuddyActionService~ split (last)**

Each handler is a leaf that depends on one or two domain services.  The
dispatcher is mechanical.  This step touches the least code and carries the
lowest risk.

Each step can be tested and merged independently.  The API surface is preserved
throughout.
@end